<p>inputxml.xml</p>

<pre><code>&lt;?xml version="1.0"?&gt;
&lt;Name input="sentence"&gt;
    &lt;Words&gt;
        &lt;Z&gt;I&lt;/Z&gt;
        &lt;Z&gt;like&lt;/Z&gt;
        &lt;Z&gt;to&lt;/Z&gt;
        &lt;Z&gt;eat&lt;/Z&gt;
        &lt;Z&gt;food&lt;/Z&gt;
    &lt;/Words&gt;
&lt;/Name&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>XMLParseWords.py</p>

<pre><code>import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

XML_file = 'inputxml.xml'
tree = ET.parse(XML_file)
root = tree.getroot()
outputString = ""
for word in root.findall('./Words/Z'):
    outputString += word.text + " "
print outputString    
</code></pre>

<p>When you run this it will output <code>I like to eat food</code>. As long as you know the structure of the xml file that you are parsing it should be fairly straightforward to grab the elements that you are looking for.  I suggest first reading the pydoc that i linked earlier to get a understanding of how <code>xml.etree.ElementTree</code> works.</p>

